need to coordinate with corporate,

233
00:15:59,769 --> 00:16:04,090
national, and international initiatives.
We focused on compute governance

234
00:16:04,090 --> 00:16:08,049
as our primary technical example. Though
coordination challenges apply

235
00:16:08,049 --> 00:16:12,110
equally to data governance, model
governance, and other technical

236
00:16:12,110 --> 00:16:17,309
measures, each approach faces the same
fundamental limitation. Technical

237
00:16:17,309 --> 00:16:20,990
measures alone cannot address systemic
risks that emerge from

238
00:16:20,990 --> 00:16:24,990
competitive dynamics and global
deployment. This is why technical

239
00:16:24,990 --> 00:16:28,509
measures must be embedded within
corporate, national, and international

240
00:16:28,750 --> 00:16:33,230
governance frameworks that align
incentives with coordinated safety
standards.

241
00:16:33,870 --> 00:16:36,149
Before we talk about those, however, we we
need

242
00:16:36,149 --> 00:16:40,110
to explore broader concepts like decision
making under uncertainty,

243
00:16:40,509 --> 00:16:44,509
game theoretic, collective action
problems, and other systemic forces

244
00:16:44,590 --> 00:16:47,669
that shape the governance landscape. We
will talk about

245
00:16:47,669 --> 00:16:49,070
this in the next section.
